As noted by Andrews, Schilling and Warnaar [andrews1999a2], the Rogers--Ramanujan products $\langle q; q^5 \rangle^{-1}$ and $\langle q^2; q^5 \rangle^{-1}$ are characters of a certain $\textnormal W_2$ algebra, which they denote by $M(2, 5)_2$. We remarked that these products coincide with the $q$-coefficients of the canonical basis vectors in the septuple identity 1.3, and explained this correspondence through 1.3. A similar phenomenon occurs for the octuple (4.7) and nonuple (1.1) identities:
\begin{center}

\begin{tabular}{c|c|c|c|c}
     Algebra & Product identity & Rog.--Ram.\ type id. & 2-Var.\ generaliz. & Correspondence\\
     \hline 
     $M(2, 5)_2$ & Septuple, 1.3 & 1.5 & 1.2 & 1.3 \\
     \hline 
     $M(2, 4)_2$ & Octuple, 4.13 & 5.8& 
     5.4 & 1.4  \\
     \hline 
     $M(3, 7)_3$ 
     & Nonuple, 1.4 & [corteel2019varvec] &
     (Open question) & (Open question)
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Indeed, the $q$-coefficients in the right-hand side of the nonuple identity 1.4 coincide with the 4 characters of the $\textnormal W_3$ algebra $M(3, 7)_3$ (up to factors of $\pm 1, \pm q, \pm 2q$), and [andrews1999a2] gives $A_2$ Rogers--Ramanujan identities for 3 out of these characters (see also [warnaar2006hall]); the more recent work of Corteel and Welsh also provides the fourth identity [corteel2019varvec]. Can one complete the bottom row of the table above with an analogue of 1.3, leading to a new proof of Theorem 1.1 from [corteel2019varvec], and possibly to applications on higher-order mock theta functions?

\begin{remark}
There is also a product identity whose $q$-coefficients are the characters of $M(2, u^2 + v^2)_2$, for any relatively prime positive integers $u, v$ such that $u + v$ is odd; see our 4.3. These might be connected as in 1 to the Andrews--Gordon identities [andrews1998theory], which generalize 1.5; 1.3 would correspond to the case $(u, v) = (1, 1)$. As pointed out by Professor Ole Warnaar (private communication), the analogue of 1.2 in this case could be a summation of variants of the Andrews--Gordon identities as in [berkovich2001variants]; the difficulty lies in relating such a summation to an infinite product in a suitable $\mathcal{T}_{\mathbb{H}^{\textnormal{-}\hspace{-0.03cm}}}(f)$ space.
\end{remark}

For $\tau \in {\mathbb{H}}^+$, one has
\[
\begin{aligned}
    \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} (-1)^n \frac{q^{n^2}}{(iq; q)_n}
    &=
    \frac{\left(q; q^2\right)}{(iq; q)}
    \left(q^{10}; q^{10}\right) \left\langle q; q^{10} \right\rangle \left\langle q^8; q^{20} \right\rangle ,
    \\
    \sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} (-1)^n \frac{q^{n^2+n}}{(iq; q)_n}
    &=
    i\frac{\left(q; q^2\right)}{(iq; q)}\left(q^{10}; q^{10}\right) \left\langle q^3; q^{10} \right\rangle \left\langle q^4; q^{20} \right\rangle.
\end{aligned}
\]

Can one use 5.16 to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i).] Obtain a 5.14-type formula for the sum $\sum_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} q^{n^2} (-q; q)_n^{-2}$ (by specialization at $xy = x/y = -1$), and a 5.15-type formula for the third-order mock theta function $f(q) := \sum_{n \ge 0} q^{n^2} (-q; q)_n^{-2}$ (see, e.g., [chen2012partition])?
    \item[(ii).] Obtain relevant identities for the sum $G(y, q)$ from 5.18, via its generalization $G(x, y, q)$?
\end{itemize}
